      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields on Three-Dimensions"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cff48913-1245-519b-9fd3-fcfe8fe4a667",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on $\\mathbb R^3?$\n\nSelect all that apply.\n\n- **I.** $N(x,y,z)=4x$\n- **II.** $K(x,y,z)=4x$\n- **III.** $H(x,y,z)=\\langle 4x, y^2\\rangle$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of $\\mathbb R^3.$ A vector field on $\\mathbb R^3$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y,z)\\in S$ to a vector $F(x,y,z)\\in\\mathbb R^3.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $N(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $K(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to values in $\\mathbb R$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\n- The function $H(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on $\\mathbb R^3.$ It assigns points in $\\mathbb R^3$ to vectors in $\\mathbb R^2$ (not $\\mathbb R^3$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the listed.\"",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
